<Base title="About" description="How this map of the śāstras is written, sourced and corrected." path="/about" og="/og/home.png">
  <header class="block-head">
    <h1 class="title">Paricaya</h1>
    <p class="gloss">introduction · how this map is made</p>
  </header>
  <div class="rule-double" aria-hidden="true"></div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>The śāstras are the disciplined fields of Indian learning: grammar, logic, the schools of philosophy, poetics, mathematics, law. This site lays them out as a map you can walk through, top to bottom, in one proposed order of study: the language first, then the ways of knowing, the schools, the Veda and the applied sciences, and finally the debates. Traditional curricula varied by school, region and period; this is one map, not the only one.</p>
    <h2 class="sec">How pages are written</h2>
    <p>Each page is drafted from public sources: encyclopedias such as the Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy and the Internet Encyclopedia of Philosophy, Wikipedia, and the introductions to published translations. The sources are listed at the foot of every page, and the small numbers in the text point to them. The text is our own; quotations from primary texts name their translator.</p>
    <p>Each school is described in its own terms. The map doesn’t take sides between them, and where scholars disagree, on dates, authorship or meaning, the page says so. The map groups the schools as āstika and nāstika, by whether they accept the Veda’s authority. That is the classification of the Brahmanical tradition, kept because the original map uses it; Buddhist and Jain thinkers did not describe themselves this way.</p>
    <h2 class="sec">Draft and reviewed</h2>
    <p>Every page starts as a <b>draft</b>: sourced, but not yet checked line by line. A page is marked <b>reviewed</b> once that check is done. Treat drafts as a first reading, not the last word.</p>
    <h2 class="sec">Corrections</h2>
    <p>Every topic page ends with a “Suggest a correction” link. It opens a GitHub issue for that page; please include a source (it needs a free GitHub account). The whole site, text and code, is open at <a href="https://github.com/dhanjit/sastras">github.com/dhanjit/sastras</a>.</p>
    <h2 class="sec">Credit</h2>
    <p>The map’s structure, its study order and the short English glosses on its blocks come from the study map by Prathosh A. P., presented in the lecture <a href="https://youtu.be/hj6yzj_HlvA">Navigating Indian Śāstras</a>, which reads the tradition as a curriculum in which each layer is a prerequisite for the next. The pages here expand that map; the expansions, and any errors in them, are ours, not the map author’s.</p>
